name: sense
kind: cli
description: >
  sense — local, on-device perception CLI for macOS. One Swift binary over
  Apple's frameworks with two halves: `sense vision` (OCR with
  searchable-PDF/hOCR/Markdown output, face/body/hand/pose/barcode/rectangle/
  horizon/saliency detection, classification, document scanning, subject
  segmentation, perceptual similarity and dedupe, camera and screen capture,
  video frame ops, CoreImage transforms and filters) and `sense audio`
  (microphone recording, on-device speech-to-text, text-to-speech, playback,
  inspection, convert/trim/split/gain). Nothing leaves the machine.
visibility: public
language: swift
license: MIT
short_description: >
  On-device vision + audio CLI for macOS. OCR, detection, screen/camera capture,
  speech-to-text, TTS — one notarized binary, nothing leaves your machine.
distribution:
  homebrew: brew install wkoszek/tap/sense
  tap: https://github.com/wkoszek/homebrew-tap
# Mirrored verbatim as the GitHub and Gitea repo topics.
tags:
  - macos
  - swift
  - cli
  - command-line-tool
  - ocr
  - vision-framework
  - coreimage
  - avfoundation
  - speech-to-text
  - text-to-speech
  - transcription
  - screenshot
  - screen-capture
  - camera
  - image-processing
  - audio-processing
  - on-device
  - privacy
  - apple-silicon
  - homebrew
notes: |
  Merge of the former `vision` and `audio` projects (both archived in favour of
  this one). Same Unix conventions across both halves: stdout=data,
  stderr=status, `-` for stdin, format from extension, --json everywhere, exit
  0/1/2/3/4.

  Four modules, one executable: SenseCLI (root command), SenseCore (version +
  TCC disclaim), SenseVision, SenseAudio. The vision module cannot be named
  `Vision` — it clashes with the framework on a case-insensitive filesystem.
  Vision parses with swift-argument-parser; audio hand-rolls its flags and gets
  handed the raw argument tail.

  Requires macOS 15. Resources/Info.plist is embedded into __TEXT,__info_plist
  on the executable target and `make build` signs with a Developer ID, both
  required so mic/speech/camera/screen TCC grants work and survive rebuilds.

  Released as a notarized universal binary via `make release` (see CLAUDE.md);
  the Homebrew formula installs that prebuilt artifact rather than compiling,
  because a source build is only ad-hoc signed and TCC grants would not survive
  an upgrade. Versions are never reused — release.sh enforces it.
